State: SDD (Workflow)

Purpose:
  Define the formal task state machine used by the workflow engine.
  This is the full state vocabulary for tasks, distinct from the compact
  state symbols defined in state/state.sdd.

  State and Stage are distinct:
    Stage  — which engineering discipline is currently active (BE, QA,
             SC, etc.) — defined in workflow/stages.sdd
    State  — the task's lifecycle condition (NEW, ANALYZING, PLANNED,
             READY, IN_PROGRESS, BLOCKED, REVIEW, TESTING, SECURITY,
             VERIFYING, DONE, FAILED, CANCELLED) — defined here

  A task at stage BE with state BLOCKED means: "Backend implementation
  is the current stage, but work cannot continue because a blocker
  exists." Stage tells you WHERE you are; State tells you WHAT CONDITION
  you are in.


Owns:
  ./


Scope:
  Task states
  State transitions
  NextAction engine
  Blocker types
  Human intervention gates
  Risk levels
  Task queue engine concept


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific task instances (bax tasks/)
  Compact state symbols (bax ../state/state.sdd)
  Engineering skills (bax skills/)
  Project architecture (bax project/architecture.sdd)


States:

  NEW        — Task has been created but not yet analyzed
  ANALYZING  — Task is being analyzed (scope, dependencies, risk)
  PLANNED    — Analysis complete, plan exists, ready for implementation
  READY      — All prerequisites met, waiting for execution slot
  IN_PROGRESS— Work is actively being performed
  BLOCKED    — Work cannot continue due to an unresolved blocker
  REVIEW     — Work is under review
  TESTING    — Work is being tested (QA stage)
  SECURITY   — Work is under security validation (SC stage)
  VERIFYING  — Work is under final verification (VR stage)
  DONE       — Work is complete and verified
  FAILED     — Work has failed and requires recovery
  CANCELLED  — Work has been cancelled, will not proceed


StateTransitions:

  Default flow:

    NEW > ANALYZING > PLANNED > READY > IN_PROGRESS > REVIEW >
    TESTING > SECURITY > VERIFYING > DONE

  Backward transitions (failure/rework):

    REVIEW > IN_PROGRESS
    TESTING > IN_PROGRESS
    SECURITY > IN_PROGRESS
    VERIFYING > IN_PROGRESS

  Blocked overlay:

    ANALYZING > BLOCKED
    PLANNED > BLOCKED
    READY > BLOCKED
    IN_PROGRESS > BLOCKED
    REVIEW > BLOCKED
    TESTING > BLOCKED
    SECURITY > BLOCKED
    VERIFYING > BLOCKED

    BLOCKED > IN_PROGRESS  (when blocker is resolved)

  Failure transitions:

    ANALYZING > FAILED
    PLANNED > FAILED
    READY > FAILED
    IN_PROGRESS > FAILED
    REVIEW > FAILED
    TESTING > FAILED
    SECURITY > FAILED
    VERIFYING > FAILED

    FAILED > ANALYZING  (recovery — re-analyze failure)

  Terminal transitions:

    DONE      (terminal — task is complete)
    CANCELLED (terminal — task is abandoned)


NextAction:

  The NextAction engine computes the next valid action for a task
  based on its current state, stage, dependencies, blockers, and gates:

    Input:
      - Current state
      - Current stage
      - Dependencies and their states
      - Blockers (type, ref, description)
      - Gate status for current stage
      - Risk level
      - Priority

    Output:
      - NextAction: <human-readable description>
      - NextStage: <stage to advance to>
      - NextState: <state to transition to>
      - Blockers: <list of active blockers, or empty>

  Examples:

    State: NEW
      NextAction: analyze task scope and dependencies
      NextStage: AR
      NextState: ANALYZING

    State: ANALYZING, Stage: AR
      NextAction: complete architecture decision
      NextStage: BDD
      NextState: PLANNED

    State: IN_PROGRESS, Stage: BE
      NextAction: complete backend implementation
      NextStage: QA
      NextState: REVIEW

    State: TESTING, Stage: QA
      NextAction: fix QA finding QA-021
      NextStage: BE
      NextState: IN_PROGRESS

    State: TESTING, Stage: QA
      Blockers: DEPENDENCY on PAY-039 (IN_PROGRESS)
      NextAction: wait for PAY-039 to complete
      NextStage: QA
      NextState: BLOCKED


Blocker:

  A blocker is an explicit reason a task cannot advance. State BLOCKED
  alone is insufficient — the task MUST declare what is blocking it:

    BlockedBy:
      type: DEPENDENCY
      ref: PAY-039
      description: PAY-039 is IN_PROGRESS, this task requires its output

    BlockedBy:
      type: DECISION
      ref: DEC-021
      description: Architecture decision pending for data model

    BlockedBy:
      type: SECURITY
      ref: SC-003
      description: Security review required before deployment

  AI MUST resolve a blocker before the task can leave BLOCKED state.
  A blocker without a type or ref is incomplete and MUST be rejected.


BlockerTypes:

  DEPENDENCY  — another task or artifact is not yet ready
  SECURITY    — a security finding or review is pending
  TECHNICAL   — a technical impediment (bug, infrastructure, environment)
  ENVIRONMENT — an environment issue (deployment, config, access)
  HUMAN       — a human decision or action is required
  EXTERNAL    — an external system or vendor is unavailable
  DECISION    — a decision is pending (bax ../decisions/)
  RESOURCE    — a resource constraint (budget, capacity, license)


Risk:

  Every task declares its risk level:

    LOW      — minimal impact if things go wrong
    MEDIUM   — moderate impact, reversible with effort
    HIGH     — significant impact, requires careful handling
    CRITICAL — severe impact, requires human approval for execution

  Risk determines which stages and gates are activated:

    LOW:      minimal chain (BE > QA > VR)
    MEDIUM:   standard chain (AR > BE > QA > SC > VR)
    HIGH:     extended chain (AR > BDD > ARCH > DB > BE > API > FE > QA > SC > DO > VR)
    CRITICAL: full chain + human approval gates at AR, SC, VR

  AI MUST NOT execute a CRITICAL risk task without human approval.


HumanIntervention:

  Certain conditions require human intervention regardless of task state:

    Architecture change
    Database migration
    Security exception
    Production incident
    Cost increase
    Cloud migration
    Data deletion
    CRITICAL risk execution
    Repeated failure (attempts >= max)

  When human intervention is required:

    HumanApproval: REQUIRED
    Reason: <why human approval is needed>
    Gate: <which gate requires approval>


TaskQueueEngine:

  The task queue engine selects the next task to execute based on:

    Priority (P0 > P1 > P2 > P3)
    Risk (CRITICAL > HIGH > MEDIUM > LOW)
    Dependencies (READY tasks with satisfied dependencies first)
    Business impact (higher impact first)
    Blocked state (BLOCKED tasks are deferred)
    State (READY > PLANNED > ANALYZING)

  The engine operates on the task's current state, not its stage:

    Input:  all tasks in the task registry
    Filter: tasks in READY, PLANNED, or ANALYZING state
            with no unresolved blockers
            with all dependencies satisfied
    Sort:   by Priority desc, Risk desc, BusinessImpact desc
    Output: NextTask: <task ID>

  AI MUST re-evaluate the queue after every state change.


StateMachine:

  Formal state machine definition:

    States:    NEW, ANALYZING, PLANNED, READY, IN_PROGRESS, BLOCKED,
               REVIEW, TESTING, SECURITY, VERIFYING, DONE, FAILED,
               CANCELLED

    Transitions:
      NEW > ANALYZING
      ANALYZING > PLANNED
      ANALYZING > BLOCKED
      ANALYZING > FAILED
      PLANNED > READY
      PLANNED > BLOCKED
      PLANNED > FAILED
      READY > IN_PROGRESS
      READY > BLOCKED
      READY > FAILED
      IN_PROGRESS > REVIEW
      IN_PROGRESS > BLOCKED
      IN_PROGRESS > FAILED
      REVIEW > TESTING
      REVIEW > IN_PROGRESS
      REVIEW > BLOCKED
      REVIEW > FAILED
      TESTING > SECURITY
      TESTING > IN_PROGRESS
      TESTING > BLOCKED
      TESTING > FAILED
      SECURITY > VERIFYING
      SECURITY > IN_PROGRESS
      SECURITY > BLOCKED
      SECURITY > FAILED
      VERIFYING > DONE
      VERIFYING > IN_PROGRESS
      VERIFYING > BLOCKED
      VERIFYING > FAILED
      BLOCKED > IN_PROGRESS
      FAILED > ANALYZING
      DONE (terminal)
      CANCELLED (terminal)

  AI MUST NOT transition a task to a state not listed above without
  creating a decision (bax PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision, ../decisions/
  decisions.sdd).

  Compact symbols (bax ../state/state.sdd) MAY be used for inline state
  notation, but the full state names above are the canonical vocabulary
  for task files and the state machine.


Rules:

  [ST1]  A task MUST declare exactly one state at any given time.

  [ST2]  A task MUST NOT transition directly between non-adjacent states
         — AI MUST follow the declared transitions above.

  [ST3]  BLOCKED is an overlay state, not a stage — when the blocker is
         resolved, the task returns to its previous state, not directly
         to IN_PROGRESS.

  [ST4]  A task MUST declare its blockers when entering BLOCKED state.

  [ST5]  A task MUST NOT leave BLOCKED state without resolving or
         removing all blockers.

  [ST6]  DONE is a terminal state — a DONE task MUST NOT re-enter the
         workflow unless reopened by human decision.

  [ST7]  CANCELLED is a terminal state — a CANCELLED task MUST NOT
         re-enter the workflow.

  [ST8]  FAILED MUST trigger failure analysis before recovery — AI MUST
         NOT silently retry without identifying the failure cause.

  [ST9]  Recovery from FAILED MUST return to ANALYZING, not directly to
         IN_PROGRESS — the failure must be analyzed first.

  [ST10] A CRITICAL risk task MUST receive human approval before
         entering IN_PROGRESS state.

  [ST11] The NextAction engine MUST be recalculated after every state
         transition.

  [ST12] The task queue engine MUST be recalculated after every state
         change that affects queue ordering.

  [ST13] State changes MUST be recorded as runtime events (bax
         ../runtime/events.sdd).

  [ST14] State history MUST be append-only — AI MUST NOT modify or
         delete past state transitions.


